# .continuerules — ICS Offshore & Energy ERP Project

## About this project

This is an internal ERP/inspection-management system for the Offshore & Energy
Department of Iranian Classification Society (ICS). It manages Third-Party
Inspection (TPI), Marine Warranty Survey (MWS), and Third-Party Engineering
Review (TPER) workflows: contracts, inspectors, checklists, equipment records,
and certification.

## Tech stack

- Frontend: React + TypeScript, Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) architecture
- Local storage: Dexie / IndexedDB (with in-memory cache for sync method compatibility)
- Backend: FastAPI (Python), async SQLAlchemy ORM, Alembic migrations
- Data layer: Supabase (equipment taxonomy: 15 disciplines, ~80 categories, ~1,000 items)
- Checklist system: 13 fixed inspection methods (Pre-Inspection Meeting, Document
  Review, Visual Inspection, Dimensional Inspection, Marking/ID Verification,
  Functional Verification, Performance Verification, Quantity, Sampling, NDT,
  PMI, Laboratory Test, Hydrostatic Test)

## Coding conventions

- Follow existing Feature-Sliced Design boundaries (app / pages / widgets /
  features / entities / shared). Do not add cross-layer imports that violate FSD.
- Avoid introducing new `any` types. The codebase already has a known technical-debt
  backlog of `any` usages — do not add to it. Prefer explicit interfaces/types.
- Do not create new monolithic "God Components." If a component would exceed
  ~200-300 lines, propose splitting it before writing it.
- There are currently three conflicting `PermissionGuard` implementations in the
  codebase. Before touching permission logic, ask which one is canonical rather
  than assuming or creating a fourth.
- Many FSD directories are empty scaffolding, not yet implemented. Don't assume a
  directory is unused just because it looks sparse — check for re-exports first.
- React Router is not fully implemented yet. Don't assume routing conventions
  exist unless you've verified them in the current code.

## Working style

- Before large refactors, briefly outline the plan and wait for confirmation
  rather than making sweeping multi-file changes in one pass.
- Prefer minimal, targeted diffs over rewriting whole files, unless a rewrite
  was explicitly requested.
- When unsure whether a change affects the Supabase schema or FastAPI backend
  contracts, ask before modifying — this is a full-stack system and frontend
  changes can silently break backend assumptions (e.g. equipment ID types).
- Client-facing and professional deliverables (docs, comments in shared code)
  should be in English. Internal notes and commit messages can be in Persian
  or English — match whatever style is already in the surrounding file.

## Authorization / permissions work

Before creating, modifying, or reviewing anything under
`shared/authorization/ui/elements/`, or anything that checks user access,
read `AUTHORIZATION.md` in full.

Gate Principle (summary — see AUTHORIZATION.md for the full test and
examples): do not create a new permission element unless there is a real
scenario where a user holds the parent permission but must be blocked from
this specific child. If access is already meaningfully decided by a parent
element (e.g. an "add" action gate), do not fragment further underneath it.
Financial figures, destructive actions, and cross-entity visibility usually
DO need their own element; form fields and buttons inside an already-gated
flow usually do NOT.

## What NOT to touch without explicit confirmation

- Do not modify equipment taxonomy data, checklist templates, or seed data files.
- Do not run destructive database migrations or drop/alter Supabase tables.
- Do not change `.env` files or expose secrets in code or logs.
- Do not delete the ~150 empty FSD scaffold directories without asking — some
  may be intentional placeholders for planned features.

